.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt I915 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm i915
.Nd Intel Haswell graphics driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd rach2.i915
.Cd rach2.i915.disable_display
.Cd rach2.i915.firstlight
.Cd rach2.i915fake
.Pp
.In drm/drm.h
.In drm/i915_drm.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dri/cardN
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dri/renderD Ns Ar M
.Pp
.Pa /sys/class/drm/cardN/gt_*_freq_mhz
.Pp
.Pa /sys/class/drm/cardN/power/rc6_*
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver binds the graphics device of an Intel Haswell processor with GT2
graphics, the HD 4400 of the Lenovo G50-70 (PCI 8086:0a16) among them:
the twenty device ids that Linux 6.12 gives the same device data.
It binds function 0 at 00:02.0 only, as Linux does.
.Pp
The driver binds a device only when the kernel command line has
.Cm rach2.i915 .
Without it the kernel logs the device and leaves the screen to the
firmware's framebuffer.
.Cm i915.modeset=0 ,
or
.Cm nomodeset
unless an
.Cm i915.modeset
word gives another value than \-1, keeps the driver off, the fake
device's included, as on Linux, and the kernel logs
.Dq i915: KMS disabled ;
the last word whose value is an integer counts.
.Pp
With
.Cm rach2.i915.disable_display
as well, as Linux's
.Ql i915.disable_display ,
the driver binds the device without its display: the device offers no
mode setting, and the display, the backlight and the firmware's
framebuffer, on which the console keeps drawing, stay as the firmware left
them.
Unlike Linux, which removes the firmware's framebuffer device in any case,
the driver leaves it, so that a trial of the GPU leaves the screen alone.
.Pp
With
.Cm rach2.i915fake
the driver also binds, for tests in QEMU, each function of QEMU's
.Ql edu
device (PCI 1234:11e8) with a fake chip behind it in place of the
hardware: the fake answers as the Lenovo's HD 4400, so the function's
.Pa /sys
entries report 8086:0a16, revision 0b, board 17aa:380c and class
0x030000, and libdrm and Mesa find a Haswell GT2; its rings complete each
command as it is handed over, without reading batches; its message is a
real MSI of the edu function; it has no stolen memory and no picture the
firmware lit, and its DDI A detects no panel, so it has no eDP
connector; and its aperture is
memory the fake fills from each page the GTT maps there and writes back
when it syncs, detiled through the fence registers as the chip would.
Its file
.Pa /sys/kernel/debug/dri/DEVICE/i915_fake
injects faults, as
.Xr debugfs 5
describes.
.Pp
A bound device is a DRM device, as
.Xr drm 4
describes, registered as
.Ql i915
with Linux's identity: description
.Ql Intel Graphics ,
version 1.6.0 and date 20230929, on the PCI bus.
It has a card node and a render node, and the features
.Dv DRIVER_GEM ,
.Dv DRIVER_RENDER ,
.Dv DRIVER_SYNCOBJ
and
.Dv DRIVER_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE ,
and
.Dv DRIVER_MODESET
and
.Dv DRIVER_ATOMIC
once its display is made, as
.Sx Display
describes; a device whose display cannot be made has neither, as Linux's
i915 on a device without display.
.Pp
At bind the driver maps the registers, the first 2 MiB of BAR 0,
uncached; turns on memory decoding and bus mastering; masks, disables and
clears every interrupt source of the display engine, the GT, power
management and the south display; then takes a vector and enables the
device's MSI message on it, and turns the display engine's master enable
on with every source still masked.
A machine without message vectors leaves the device without interrupts;
the driver then polls for its work.
.Pp
It reads the firmware's description of the outputs and the panel: the
ACPI OpRegion at the address in the ASLS register, and the video BIOS
table it holds, or else the one in the option ROM's shadow at 0xc0000.
The kernel logs
.Dq i915: opregion
with its version and mailboxes, and
.Dq i915: vbt
with the table's signature, source, version and number of outputs, or that
there is none and each port takes Linux's defaults.
.Pp
It then starts its four rings, the render ring, the blitter ring, the
video ring and the video enhancement ring: for each a status page and a
ring of 16 KiB in the global GTT, the ring stopped, emptied and started
again with every wait bounded.
Each ring's user interrupt is enabled and stays masked except while the
driver waits for that ring's work, the video enhancement ring's among the
power management events as on Linux; every other source stays masked.
A ring that does not start is logged, and the device takes no work.
Before the rings start the driver writes Linux's workarounds for Haswell
to the GT and to the render engine, and writes them again after a reset
that loses them; it logs
.Dq i915: workarounds: applied
with their numbers, and
.Dq i915: workarounds: NAME: lost
for one the chip did not keep.
.Pp
With the rings comes the aliasing PPGTT, as Linux runs it on Haswell: one
table of the GPU's addresses that every context of every program shares,
which maps each buffer bound in the global GTT at the same address and
none of the driver's own objects.
A batch that is not privileged runs in it, so every program's batches can
read and write every buffer any program has bound, as on Linux.
The kernel logs
.Dq i915: ppgtt: aliasing
with its size and the directory's place.
.Pp
With
.Cm rach2.i915.firstlight
the render ring writes one value to a page of the global GTT before any
display code runs, and the kernel logs
.Dq i915: first light: ok
or the failure by name: no room in the ring, the submission refused, no
breadcrumb, the value read back, or no user interrupt.
.Pp
The GT sleeps in RC6 while it is idle, with Linux's thresholds: under the
hardware's timers after 64 ms without work, and at once while the driver
holds it parked.
Its frequency moves in steps of 50 MHz between RPn and RP0, the chip's
limits, which the bind reads with the efficient frequency RPe from the
chip's power controller.
While the GT has work the chip's up and down interrupts move the
frequency, in steps that double while they go one way: up when the GT was
busy for more than 95% of an interval of 16 ms at low frequencies, 13 ms
between and 10 ms near RP0; down when it was busy for less than 85% of
32 ms; and on the down timeout, to RPe and then RPn.
Parked, the GT runs at RPn.
A request unparks it, and it parks again at the driver's retire pass,
once a second, that finds every ring idle and its requests retired, so
that a client's next frame finds the GT awake at the frequency its last
one left, as Linux's did from one call of a GL round trip to the next.
The bind leaves the GT parked, and logs
.Dq i915: rps:
with RPn, RP1, RP0, RPe and the highest frequency allowed.
.Pp
The kernel logs
.Dq i915: found
with the device's place, id, subplatform and revision, and
.Dq i915: registered
with its card.
.Sh IOCTLS
The core's ioctls are those of
.Xr drm 4 .
The driver's own,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_*
from
.Dv DRM_COMMAND_BASE ,
fall in the families below.
.Ss Identity and parameters
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_VERSION
answers as above.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GETPARAM
answers each parameter as Linux 6.12 answers it on this device: the
device id, the revision, the rings that run (render, blitter, video and
video enhancement), the LLC, write-through eDRAM, the slice,
subslice and EU counts the fuses give, the command streamer's timestamp
frequency of 12.5 MHz, and the features of the buffer and submission calls.
.Dv I915_PARAM_CMD_PARSER_VERSION
is 10, the version of Linux's command parser, which every ring runs.
A parameter Linux answers with
.Er ENODEV
on Haswell, such as
.Dv I915_PARAM_IRQ_ACTIVE ,
.Dv I915_PARAM_HUC_STATUS
or
.Dv I915_PARAM_PXP_STATUS ,
fails with it; an unknown one fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
and a value that cannot be written with
.Er EFAULT .
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_SETPARAM
is not served and fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_QUERY
answers each item in its own length, which is the size of the answer, or
the item's error, as Linux does:
.Dv DRM_I915_QUERY_TOPOLOGY_INFO ,
one slice of two subslices and their EUs;
.Dv DRM_I915_QUERY_ENGINE_INFO ,
.Li rcs0 ,
.Li bcs0 ,
.Li vcs0
and
.Li vecs0 ;
.Dv DRM_I915_QUERY_MEMORY_REGIONS ,
system memory alone, of the size of the machine's memory.
.Dv DRM_I915_QUERY_PERF_CONFIG ,
.Dv DRM_I915_QUERY_HWCONFIG_BLOB ,
.Dv DRM_I915_QUERY_GEOMETRY_SUBSLICES
and
.Dv DRM_I915_QUERY_GUC_SUBMISSION_VERSION
are
.Er ENODEV ,
and an unknown item
.Er EINVAL .
.Ss Buffers
Buffer objects follow Linux's i915 on an LLC part.
Their pages are anonymous memory, written back, and are bound into the
global GTT when the aperture or the GPU needs them.
These calls are served on the card node and the render node alike:
.Bl -tag -width DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_GET_APERTURE
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CREATE
An object of
.Fa size
bytes rounded up to a page, and its handle.
.Er EINVAL
for 0 or a size that rounds up past 2^64, and
.Er E2BIG
past what an object holds.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CREATE_EXT
The object of GEM_CREATE, after the chain of extensions at
.Fa extensions .
.Dv I915_GEM_CREATE_EXT_MEMORY_REGIONS
names the regions it may be placed in, of which there is one, system
memory, class
.Dv I915_MEMORY_CLASS_SYSTEM
instance 0.
.Dv I915_GEM_CREATE_EXT_PROTECTED_CONTENT
and
.Dv I915_GEM_CREATE_EXT_SET_PAT
fail with
.Er ENODEV .
.Er EINVAL
for a flag, since
.Dv I915_GEM_CREATE_EXT_FLAG_NEEDS_CPU_ACCESS
needs a second region; for an unknown extension, or one whose flags or
reserved words are not 0; and for a region list that is empty, longer than
seven, names a region twice or one the device lacks, or comes twice;
.Er E2BIG
past 512 extensions;
.Er EFAULT
for an address that cannot be read; then GEM_CREATE's errors.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_PREAD , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_PWRITE
Copy bytes out of the object or into it, after its work is done.
PREAD reads through the processor's view; PWRITE writes through the
aperture when the object's caching would need the processor's lines
flushed, and otherwise through the processor's view.
An object imported from another device has no pages of its own and is
read and written through the aperture.
.Er EINVAL
past the object's end,
.Er EFAULT
for a buffer outside the caller's memory.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_SET_DOMAIN , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_SW_FINISH
Move the object into the CPU, GTT or WC domain before the caller touches a
map of it, flushing and invalidating what the move needs; SW_FINISH
flushes the processor's writes of an object that is scanned out.
.Er EINVAL
for a GPU domain or a write domain other than the read domains.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_MMAP_OFFSET
The offset to give
.Xr mmap 2
on the node for a map of the kind
.Fa flags
names:
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_GTT
through the aperture, detiled by a fence;
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_WB
the object's pages written back;
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_WC
and
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_UC
write-combining and uncached.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_MMAP_GTT
shares the call's number and gives the GTT kind.
.Er ENODEV
for WC and UC without PAT, for
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_FIXED ,
and for a map of an import's pages;
.Er EINVAL
for an extension, since none is defined.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_MMAP
Map
.Fa size
bytes of the object's pages from
.Fa offset
into the caller, shared, readable and writable, and give the address in
.Fa addr_ptr ,
as Linux's call does: written back, or write-combining under
.Dv I915_MMAP_WC .
The map holds the object until it is unmapped.
.Er EINVAL
for another flag, and for an offset or size outside the object, an offset
not a multiple of the page or a size of 0;
.Er ENODEV
for
.Dv I915_MMAP_WC
without PAT;
.Er ENXIO
for an import or a userptr object.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_SET_TILING , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_GET_TILING
Set or read the object's layout, linear, X or Y, its stride, and the
swizzle of bit 6 programs must apply to address a tiled object's pages:
on Haswell none, or 9 and 10 for X tiles and 9 for Y when the memory
controller interleaves its two channels.
A stride must be a multiple of the tile's width, 512 bytes for X and 128
for Y, and at most 256 KiB.
GTT maps of the object fault again after a change.
.Er EBUSY
for another layout of an object a framebuffer is made of.
A framebuffer made by
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_ADDFB2
without modifiers of an X-tiled object is X-tiled, and of a Y-tiled one
is refused with
.Er EINVAL ,
as is a modifier other than the object's tiling, a pitch other than its
stride, or X tiles that do not fit the object in whole rows.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_SET_CACHING , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_GET_CACHING
Set or read the object's level in the processor's caches:
.Dv I915_CACHING_NONE ,
.Dv I915_CACHING_CACHED ,
or
.Dv I915_CACHING_DISPLAY ,
which Haswell's GT2 serves uncached.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_BUSY , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_WAIT
Whether engines still read or write the object, and a wait for them of
.Fa timeout_ns ,
for ever when it is negative, with the time left written back;
.Er ETIME
when the object is still busy at the timeout.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_THROTTLE
Wait for the caller's requests that were submitted more than 20 ms ago,
so that a client keeps at most 20 ms of work queued.
.Er EIO
when the GPU is wedged,
.Er EINTR
when a signal comes.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_GET_APERTURE
The global GTT's size in
.Fa aper_size ,
2 GiB on Haswell's GT2, and in
.Fa aper_available_size
what is left of it once the pinned bindings and the kernel's own are
taken away.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_MADVISE
Mark the object's pages as needed,
.Dv I915_MADV_WILLNEED ,
or not,
.Dv I915_MADV_DONTNEED ,
and set
.Fa retained
to whether its pages are still there.
When memory runs short, the kernel frees the pages of an idle object
marked DONTNEED of which no dma-buf exists, unbinding it and revoking its
maps; such an object is purged, keeps that state, and fails with
.Er EFAULT
on each later use of its pages, and a touch of a map of it raises
.Dv SIGBUS .
An object marked DONTNEED that has no pages is purged at once.
.Er EINVAL
for any other state.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_USERPTR
An object over
.Fa user_size
bytes of the caller's memory at
.Fa user_ptr ,
both whole pages, and its handle.
Its pages are pinned on its first use.
An unmap, a change of protection, a fork or a copy-on-write break revokes
the bindings; a busy GPU keeps its frames until its work ends.
Each pinned shared disk page also keeps its mapped file until then, so
writeback includes the device's final stores after unmap.
Its next use pins the range again, or fails with
.Er EFAULT
once they are unmapped.
Only the address space that made it may pin them.
A range that starts below the caller's memory, at 0 among others, makes an
object, as on Linux, whose first use fails with
.Er EFAULT .
.Dv I915_USERPTR_PROBE
asks that the whole range be memory with pages now.
PREAD and PWRITE fail on it with
.Er EINVAL ,
a map of it with
.Er ENODEV ,
and SET_TILING and every caching but
.Dv I915_CACHING_CACHED
with
.Er ENXIO ;
PRIME does not export it.
.Er EINVAL
for an unknown flag, no bytes, or a pointer or size not of whole pages;
.Er E2BIG
for a size past what an object holds;
.Er EFAULT
for a range that ends past user space, or with PROBE one not wholly in
memory with pages;
.Er ENODEV
for
.Dv I915_USERPTR_READ_ONLY ,
since Haswell's GTT has no read-only entries, and for
.Dv I915_USERPTR_UNSYNCHRONIZED ,
which Linux refuses too.
.El
.Pp
A fault on a GTT map binds the object inside the aperture whole when it
fits, or else a view of at least 1 MiB around the page in whole tile
rows, and gives it one of the 32 fence registers, the least recently used
that no fault holds.
A binding that finds no room in the aperture or the GTT takes the place of
idle bindings, the least recently used first; one whose object the GPU
still uses stays, and the fault waits for the GPU to finish with it until
a signal comes.
The fault never waits for a binding the GPU uses above the aperture: it
binds a view of the whole object beside it.
A map whose binding or fence is taken away faults again on its next
touch; one that finds no room or no fence yet, or whose wait a signal
ends, returns to the program, which handles the signal and touches the
page again, as on Linux.
An allocation of an object's pages that finds no memory fails with
.Er ENOMEM .
.Pp
The core's PRIME calls export an object as a dma-buf, which
.Dv DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC
moves into the CPU domain and back to the GTT's, and import another
device's dma-buf as an object.
All of these calls fail with
.Er ENOENT
for a handle that names no object.
.Ss Contexts
A context is a file's own state of the GPU: on the render ring a hardware
image of 17 pages, which the chip saves when it switches away and restores
when it switches back, made from a default image the driver records once
with Linux's context workarounds; the blitter keeps no image.
Each open file has the default context, 0; the others are numbered from 1,
the lowest free number first, and are freed with the file.
A context's parameters can be set until its first use, which fixes them.
.Bl -tag -width DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CONTEXT_SETPARAM
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CONTEXT_CREATE , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CONTEXT_CREATE_EXT
A new context and its id.
With
.Dv I915_CONTEXT_CREATE_FLAGS_USE_EXTENSIONS ,
the chain of extensions at
.Fa extensions
is read, at most 512 deep
.Pq Er E2BIG ;
each
.Dv I915_CONTEXT_CREATE_EXT_SETPARAM
sets a parameter as SETPARAM does, and
.Dv I915_CONTEXT_CREATE_EXT_CLONE
or an unknown extension is
.Er EINVAL .
.Dv I915_CONTEXT_CREATE_FLAGS_SINGLE_TIMELINE
and unknown flags are
.Er EINVAL .
A file banned for hanging the GPU gets
.Er EIO .
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CONTEXT_DESTROY
Destroys a context; its image stays until the chip has switched away from
it.
.Er ENOENT
for context 0 or no such context.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CONTEXT_GETPARAM
.Dv I915_CONTEXT_PARAM_GTT_SIZE ,
the global GTT's size;
.Dv BANNABLE ,
.Dv RECOVERABLE
and
.Dv PERSISTENCE ,
1 by default;
.Dv NO_ERROR_CAPTURE ,
.Dv PRIORITY
and
.Dv PROTECTED_CONTENT ;
and
.Dv SSEU ,
the slices, subslices and EUs of an engine, which a size of 0 answers with
the structure's size.
.Dv VM
is
.Er ENODEV ,
since there is no full PPGTT.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CONTEXT_SETPARAM
.Dv NO_ERROR_CAPTURE
and
.Dv RECOVERABLE ;
.Dv BANNABLE ,
which only a privileged process may clear
.Pq Er EPERM ;
.Dv PERSISTENCE ,
which cannot be cleared without preemption
.Pq Er ENODEV ;
and, before the first use,
.Dv ENGINES ,
a map of up to 64 engines by class and instance, holes allowed, whose
.Dv I915_CONTEXT_ENGINES_EXT_LOAD_BALANCE
is
.Er ENODEV ,
.Dv I915_CONTEXT_ENGINES_EXT_BOND
is checked and has no effect, and
.Dv I915_CONTEXT_ENGINES_EXT_PARALLEL_SUBMIT
is
.Er EINVAL ,
as on Linux without GuC.
.Dv PRIORITY ,
.Dv SSEU
and
.Dv VM
are
.Er ENODEV .
.El
.Pp
Each call takes Linux's
.Er EINVAL
for a size, flag, pad or reserved field where none belongs,
.Er ENOENT
for no such context or an engine of the map that does not run, and
.Er EFAULT
for memory that cannot be read.
.Pp
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GET_RESET_STATS
gives a context's batches that were active at a hang and pending behind
one, and to a privileged process the resets of the device.
.Pp
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_REG_READ
reads the render ring's timestamp, at offset 0x2358, the one register
Linux allows on Haswell.
With
.Dv I915_REG_READ_8B_WA
in the offset's low bits it reads the high half again until it does not
change, so that a carry between the halves is never seen half done;
without it the low half is read and then the high.
It fails with
.Er EINVAL
for another register or flag.
.Ss Submission
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_EXECBUFFER2
and
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_EXECBUFFER2_WR
run a batch on one of the four rings as Linux 6.12 does on Haswell:
.Dv I915_EXEC_DEFAULT
and
.Dv I915_EXEC_RENDER
name the render ring,
.Dv I915_EXEC_BLT
the blitter,
.Dv I915_EXEC_BSD ,
with any of its ring flags since Haswell has one video ring, the video
ring, and
.Dv I915_EXEC_VEBOX
the video enhancement ring.
The objects are bound in the global GTT, at the offset each entry
presumed when it is free, at a pinned offset under
.Dv EXEC_OBJECT_PINNED ,
or elsewhere, evicting what the GPU no longer uses and at last waiting
for it to idle; an object that moved has its offset written back with
bit 0 set.
Relocations by handle, or by index under
.Dv I915_EXEC_HANDLE_LUT ,
are written unless the presumed offset was right, and their presumed
offsets written back.
.Dv I915_EXEC_NO_RELOC ,
.Dv I915_EXEC_BATCH_FIRST ,
.Dv EXEC_OBJECT_WRITE ,
.Dv EXEC_OBJECT_ASYNC ,
.Dv EXEC_OBJECT_NEEDS_GTT ,
.Dv EXEC_OBJECT_SUPPORTS_48B_ADDRESS
and
.Dv EXEC_OBJECT_PAD_TO_SIZE
mean what they mean on Linux.
A batch is ordered after the last writer of each object it uses, unless
the object is
.Dv EXEC_OBJECT_ASYNC ,
after the sync file of
.Dv I915_EXEC_FENCE_IN ,
after the submission of the request of
.Dv I915_EXEC_FENCE_SUBMIT ,
and after each syncobj of
.Dv I915_EXEC_FENCE_ARRAY
marked
.Dv I915_EXEC_FENCE_WAIT ;
.Dv I915_EXEC_FENCE_OUT
returns a sync file in the upper half of
.Fa rsvd2 ,
and each syncobj marked
.Dv I915_EXEC_FENCE_SIGNAL
takes the batch's fence.
Under
.Dv I915_EXEC_USE_EXTENSIONS ,
.Fa cliprects_ptr
holds a chain of extensions instead of the fence array, of which
.Dv DRM_I915_GEM_EXECBUFFER_EXT_TIMELINE_FENCES
gives syncobjs with a point each, 0 for a binary one: the batch is
ordered after the fence of each point it names, a point already signalled
and gone is passed over, and a point marked
.Dv I915_EXEC_FENCE_SIGNAL
is added to its timeline with the batch's fence.
As on Linux, the batch is also ordered after the fence of a syncobj
named with neither flag, and of one to signal.
A fence of another driver is waited for 10 seconds at most, Linux's
default; the batch then runs, and its own fence ends with
.Er ETIMEDOUT .
.Dv I915_EXEC_GEN7_SOL_RESET
clears the four streamout write offsets on the render ring.
.Pp
The call fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a bad flag, count, offset, alignment, relocation or engine, for
.Dv I915_EXEC_FENCE_IN
with
.Dv I915_EXEC_FENCE_SUBMIT ,
for an unknown extension, extensions with the fence array, a syncobj to
wait for with no fence or at a point its timeline has not reached, a
timeline point both waited for and signalled, and for
.Dv I915_EXEC_GEN7_SOL_RESET
on the blitter;
.Er ENOENT
for no such object, context or syncobj, or a closed context;
.Er EPERM
for
.Dv I915_EXEC_SECURE
from other than a privileged master;
.Er EINVAL
for a batch with a length that the command parser cannot read, as below;
.Er ENODEV
for such a batch in an object imported from another device;
.Er ENOSPC
when the objects do not fit in the GTT;
.Er EAGAIN
when a userptr object's pages changed;
.Er EFAULT
for memory that cannot be read; and
.Er EIO
once the GPU is wedged or the context is banned.
.Pp
A batch that does not finish is found by the driver's heartbeat, which
beats every 2.5 seconds while the GPU is busy, and its engine is reset
between 5 and 7.5 seconds after the batch reached it, or the whole GPU
when the engine does not reset.
The parameters
.Pa reset
and
.Pa enable_hangcheck
in debugfs change this, as
.Xr debugfs 5
describes.
The batch's out-fence ends with
.Er EIO ,
and the batches behind it run; after a reset of the whole GPU those of
the other engines run again with
.Er EAGAIN
on their fences.
A context is banned at its third hang within 120 seconds, or at its first
when it is not recoverable.
A file scores 3 for each ban and 1 for a hang within a minute of its last,
and at 9 can make no more contexts.
A GPU that does not reset is wedged: every batch fails with
.Er EIO
until a later reset succeeds.
.Ss Command parser
A batch whose length the call gives,
.Fa batch_len
above 0, is checked by Linux 6.12's command parser for Haswell, whoever
the caller is; a batch without a length runs unprivileged as it is.
The parser copies the batch to a shadow the program cannot reach and
reads its commands from there.
A batch whose every command the parser's tables allow runs privileged
from the shadow, so that commands the chip refuses in an unprivileged
batch, such as
.Dv MI_LOAD_REGISTER_IMM
to the registers OpenGL and Vulkan need, take effect.
A batch with a command the tables refuse runs unprivileged from the
original, where the chip turns what it must into no-ops, and the call
succeeds: a privileged command such as
.Dv MI_USER_INTERRUPT
or
.Dv MI_SET_CONTEXT ,
a register outside the ring's allowlist, a masked register written with
a value its mask refuses or other than by
.Dv MI_LOAD_REGISTER_IMM ,
an access through the global GTT, and any
.Dv MI_BATCH_BUFFER_START ,
whose chain the chip then follows unprivileged.
The video and video enhancement rings have no allowlist, as on Linux, so
a batch there that names any register runs unprivileged.
A command of a client the ring does not run, a command that runs past the
batch's length, and a batch without
.Dv MI_BATCH_BUFFER_END
fail the call with
.Er EINVAL .
.Ss Video
The video ring runs Haswell's fixed-function decoder and encoder, and the
video enhancement ring its scaling, deinterlacing and colour conversion,
as on Linux; a program reaches them through VA-API.
The GNU root has libva, its DRM backend and Intel's i965 VA driver 2.4.1,
which libva loads for the render node, and
.Xr vainfo 1 ,
which lists what the driver offers: on Haswell, decoding of MPEG-2,
H.264, VC-1 and JPEG, encoding of MPEG-2 and H.264, and video processing.
The driver gives its batches to the video ring with
.Dv I915_EXEC_BSD
and to the video enhancement ring with
.Dv I915_EXEC_VEBOX ,
through the command parser as above.
.Ss Display
The device has three CRTCs, pipes A to C, each with a primary plane, a
sprite and a cursor, Linux's planes for Haswell; and, when DDI A detects
a panel that answers on its AUX channel, a TMDS encoder on DDI A and the
eDP connector
.Ql eDP-1 ,
always connected, with the modes of the panel's EDID and its fixed modes,
and the properties
.Ql scaling mode ,
Full aspect by default, Full or Center, and
.Ql max bpc ,
6 to 12.
At bind the driver reads out what the firmware left lit, without a write:
on the Lenovo pipe A drives the panel through the eDP transcoder at
.Dq 1366x768 :
60 76300 1366 1374 1384 1558 768 771 772 816, 18 bits a pixel on one lane
at 270 MHz.
It adopts that pipe into the atomic state, on, with the panel's mode,
routed to the connector and showing the firmware's framebuffer, and logs
.Dq i915: display: pipe A adopted
with the mode, the link and the framebuffer.
.Pp
A commit that keeps a pipe's mode and the connector's properties changes
the planes alone; one that changes them, or turns a pipe on or off, sets
the mode in Linux's order, with the panel's power, its link trained and
its backlight, and logs
.Dq i915: display: pipe A set
with the sizes, the clock, the bits a pixel and the link.
Any pipe may drive the panel.
A mode of the panel's size and refresh rate is driven as it is; a
different size is scaled to the panel by the pipe's fitter as
.Ql scaling mode
says, and fails with
.Er EINVAL
when the source is wider than the fitter takes.
A mode fails with
.Er EINVAL
when its refresh rate is more than a hertz from the panel's, when no link
carries it at 18 bits a pixel, or when its pixel rate passes the CD
clock.
A link that fails to train is set lower, the connector's
.Ql link-status
goes
.Dq Bad
and a hotplug event asks the compositor to set the mode again, which
trains the new link and sets the status
.Dq Good .
Turning a pipe off turns its planes, backlight, transcoder, link and the
panel's power off.
.Pp
DDI B, C and D, each as the VBT names it and the chip's straps report it,
have a TMDS encoder that reaches every pipe and a
.Ql DP- Ns Ar n
connector, an
.Ql HDMI-A- Ns Ar n
connector, or both on a DP++ port; on the Lenovo DDI B is
.Ql HDMI-A-1
and DDI C
.Ql DP-1
and
.Ql HDMI-A-2 .
An HDMI connector is connected when its monitor's EDID, read over GMBUS
or by bit-banging when GMBUS fails, says a digital sink; a DisplayPort
connector when the port's hotplug pin is high and the sink's DPCD reads.
Each has
.Ql max bpc ,
8 to 12 for HDMI and 6 to 12 for DisplayPort.
An HDMI mode is set at 12 or 8 bits a colour, the deepest whose TMDS
clock the port, at most 300 MHz, the adaptor and the sink carry, from a
WRPLL with Linux's dividers, with the AVI, SPD and GCP infoframes for an
HDMI sink and limited range for a CEA mode, and logs
.Dq i915: display: pipe B set
with the mode, the clock, the TMDS clock and the PLL.
A DisplayPort mode is set on the slowest link that carries it, clocked by
the LCPLL.
A commit fails with
.Er EBUSY
when it needs a third WRPLL.
Plugging or unplugging a monitor raises the PCH's hotplug interrupt; the
driver probes the port's connectors and sends a hotplug event for each
whose status or EDID changed, and probes once more a second later a
connector that did not change.
More than five long pulses on a pin within a second mask it, logging
.Dq i915: hotplug: storm on port B; polling it ;
its connectors are then polled every 10 seconds until the pin is unmasked
2 minutes later.
A connector forced on through its
.Pa status
file takes the EDID written to its
.Pa edid_override
file in place of the monitor's, as IGT's
.Sy kms_hdmi_inject
does.
A pipe the firmware lit on DDI B to D is turned off at bind.
.Pp
The primary plane takes C8, RGB565, XRGB8888, XBGR8888, XRGB2101010,
XBGR2101010 and XBGR16161616F, the sprite XRGB8888, XBGR8888, the two
2101010 formats, XRGB16161616F, XBGR16161616F, YUYV, YVYU, UYVY and VYUY,
each linear or X-tiled
.Pq Dv I915_FORMAT_MOD_X_TILED ,
and the cursor ARGB8888, linear, 64, 128 or 256 pixels wide and from 8
lines to its width high, square when turned by 180 degrees.
The planes have no
.Ql FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS
property, as on Linux before display version 12;
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_DIRTYFB
still flushes the rectangles it names.
A commit fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a modifier that
does not match the object's tiling; a stride that is not a multiple of 64
bytes, or of 512 X-tiled; a YUV source that starts or ends on an odd
pixel; scaling; and a primary plane that does not cover the CRTC.
A plane reads a stride of at most 8192 pixels, and at most 32 KiB on the
primary plane and 16 KiB on the sprite.
A framebuffer with a wider stride, up to 16384 pixels wide, is shown
through a remapped view of the GTT that holds only the tiles, or for a
linear framebuffer the pages of each row, that the plane's source
rectangle covers, as on Linux; a commit fails with
.Er EINVAL
when the framebuffer is linear and its stride is not a multiple of 4096
bytes, or when the view's own stride, the source rectangle's width in
whole tiles, still passes the plane's.
Each framebuffer shown, or its view, is bound in the GTT at a multiple
of 256 KiB; a whole framebuffer goes inside the aperture when room
there needs no wait and above it otherwise, as Linux binds it on a
chip without a GMCH.
.Pp
Each CRTC has a legacy gamma table of 256 entries for
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETGAMMA
and
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_SETGAMMA ,
the
.Ql DEGAMMA_LUT
and
.Ql GAMMA_LUT
properties of 1024 entries, which
.Ql DEGAMMA_LUT_SIZE
and
.Ql GAMMA_LUT_SIZE
give, and
.Ql CTM ,
as on Linux.
A commit fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a
.Ql DEGAMMA_LUT
of another size, a
.Ql GAMMA_LUT
of other than 1024 or 256 entries, and a C8 plane on a CRTC without a
.Ql GAMMA_LUT
of 256 entries, which is its palette, or with a
.Ql DEGAMMA_LUT .
With both tables set the pipe resamples each to 512 entries.
.Pp
Vblanks are counted from the pipe's frame counter.
A page flip's event comes at the vblank that shows the new framebuffer.
.Dv DRM_MODE_PAGE_FLIP_ASYNC
flips the primary plane between X-tiled framebuffers of the same format,
stride and place without waiting for a vblank, and its event comes when
the plane has taken the new surface.
.Pp
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GET_PIPE_FROM_CRTC_ID
gives the pipe, 0 for A, of the CRTC whose id it is given, or fails with
.Er ENOENT
for none; a render node may not call it.
Each CRTC's
.Pa i915_pipe
and
.Pa i915_current_bpc
in
.Xr debugfs 5
give the same pipe and the bits a colour the pipe runs at.
.Pp
The pipe CRC is read through
.Pa crc/control
and
.Pa crc/data ,
as
.Xr debugfs 5
describes, from the sources
.Ql plane1 ,
the primary plane,
.Ql plane2 ,
the sprite, and
.Ql pipe ,
.Ql pf
and
.Ql auto ,
the pipe's output after its panel fitter.
While a source runs on pipe A feeding the panel, the pipe's input is set
through its fitter's power well, by a mode set when the fitter is off, and
set back when the source stops, as Linux does.
The first frame after a source is set, or after a mode set, is dropped.
Each line of
.Pa crc/data
gives the frame and five values, the CRC and four zeros, as Linux's i915.
.Pp
The panel's backlight,
.Pa intel_backlight ,
is taken over from the boot's driver, as
.Xr backlight 4
describes.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/dri/renderDM
.It Pa /dev/dri/cardN
The card node of a bound device.
.It Pa /dev/dri/renderD Ns Ar M
Its render node.
.It Pa /sys/kernel/debug/dri/DEVICE/i915_*
Linux's debugfs files that IGT opens, as
.Xr debugfs 5
lists them.
.It Pa /sys/kernel/debug/dri/DEVICE/i915_fake
The fault file of a device bound to the fake chip.
.El
.Ss The card in /sys
While the card is registered its PCI function is shown bound to
.Ql i915 :
its
.Pa driver
link names
.Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/i915
and its uevent begins with
.Ql DRIVER=i915 ,
as
.Xr sysfs 5
gives them.
The card's directory,
.Pa /sys/class/drm/cardN ,
holds Linux's files of the GT's frequency, each a decimal number of MHz
and a newline:
.Bl -tag -width gt_boost_freq_mhz
.It Pa gt_act_freq_mhz
The frequency the GT runs at, as the chip reports it.
.It Pa gt_cur_freq_mhz
The frequency the driver last asked for.
.It Pa gt_boost_freq_mhz
The frequency a boost would ask for; at bind the highest frequency
allowed: RP0, or the overclocking limit of a chip that has one.
.It Pa gt_max_freq_mhz
The highest frequency the driver asks for; RP0 at bind, and up to the
overclocking limit of a chip that has one.
.It Pa gt_min_freq_mhz
The lowest frequency the driver asks for; RPn at bind.
.It Pa gt_RP0_freq_mhz , gt_RP1_freq_mhz , gt_RPn_freq_mhz
The chip's highest frequency, its guaranteed one, and its lowest.
.El
.Pp
The boost, max and min files are written by root with a number in
decimal, in hexadecimal after
.Ql 0x
or in octal after
.Ql 0 ,
and at most one newline, as Linux's
.Fn kstrtou32
reads it; the number is rounded to the nearest 50 MHz.
A write fails with
.Er EINVAL
when it is no number, when it lies outside RPn and the highest frequency
allowed, or when it would put max below min or min above max, and with
.Er ERANGE
when it does not fit in 32 bits.
A new max or min moves the frequency into the new limits at once.
.Pp
The card's file
.Pa error ,
mode 0600, gives the first error state captured at a hang, in the text of
.Pa i915_error_state ,
or
.Ql No error state collected ;
any write clears it, as IGT does before a test of hangs.
.Pp
Below it,
.Pa power
holds RC6's files:
.Bl -tag -width rc6_residency_ms
.It Pa power/rc6_enable
The RC6 states enabled, in hexadecimal: 1 for RC6, 0 without it.
.It Pa power/rc6_residency_ms
The milliseconds the GT has spent in RC6 since the bind, in decimal,
wrapping at 2^32.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vainfo 1 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr backlight 4 ,
.Xr drm 4 ,
.Xr watchdog 4 ,
.Xr debugfs 5
.Sh HISTORY
The driver follows Linux 6.12's i915, whose register names and device
table it takes.
